--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/worker-batches.md
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# Prepared worker sessions
+
+`blot_host::worker_sessions::Workers` is a separate optional host API for coarse
+jobs sharing immutable prepared data. Construct it with a module exporting a
+callback service (Ink's `pen/worker_session.serve` is one adapter). Each worker
+retains its own guest and prepared context. `run(revision, context, inputs)`
+checks exact context bits when reusing the current revision, dispatches jobs
+round-robin, and returns results in submission order. A changed revision sends
+context once to each worker used; `reset` forces preparation even if the next
+revision is equal. A trap, bad response or early return discards that worker's
+guest before processing its next job. Failed jobs occupy their original result
+slots. No worker sees the live game world.
+
+The callback handshake is `[-4670546]`. Host commands are `[0]` (close),
+`[1, context...]` (prepare, acknowledged with `[1]`), and `[2, input...]`
+(plan, answered with `[2, output...]`). Arrays contain finite F32 values and at
+most 16384 payload lanes. Pools have 1–8 workers, at most 32 jobs per batch,
+and a 64 MiB memory limit per guest. The owner closes or drops the pool to join
+all threads; it also owns invalidation on restore, reload and resource changes.
+This API is not yet connected to the native game's packet loop or the browser.
+
+`qualify_sessions MODULE.wasm OUT.json` checks the Ink worker fixture against
+brute-force nearest-point results, including reset and failure recovery. Its
+measurements compare fresh workers, one/four prepared workers, and serial work
+that rebuilds context once per batch. They do not predict a whole-game gain.
